<section>
  <p class="kicker">See and avoid</p>
  <h1>What a pilot can see.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    In VFR flight, the pilot is responsible for avoiding a collision: <em>&ldquo;nothing in this
    Regulation shall relieve the pilot-in-command of an aircraft from the
    responsibility of taking such action &hellip; as will best avert
    collision&rdquo;</em>. In French airspace, VFR flights are not separated from one
    another. Air traffic control sees aircraft carrying a transponder, which is mandatory
    in class A to D airspace, and passes traffic information. That does not
    provide separation. Under a flight information service &mdash; a SIV in France,
    where most light aviation flies &mdash; carrying a transponder is optional.
  </p>
  <p>
    That leaves the pilot&rsquo;s eye. Published figures cover its field of
    view, the sector to scan, how long a scan cycle takes and the time
    available to react. This page brings them together, then looks at what an ADS-L device
    adds.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The four angles</h2>
  <h3>&ldquo;A pilot sees 190&deg;&rdquo; is true. It is the first of four numbers.</h3>

  <p>
    Four different angles are used interchangeably when lookout is discussed.
    They measure different things.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What is measured</th>
          <th>Angle</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Field of view, head still</th>
          <td>&asymp; 190&ndash;200&deg; wide, 120&ndash;135&deg; high</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>What the cockpit leaves visible</th>
          <td>a forward arc &mdash; the wing, the door and window posts, the nose, the panel and the visor block parts of it. The airframe blocks the view aft</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>The scan pilots are taught</th>
          <td>60&deg; left + 60&deg; right, 10&deg; up and down &mdash; in blocks of 10 to 15&deg;, about a second each, nine to twelve blocks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Where the eye identifies an object</th>
          <td>&asymp; 10&ndash;15&deg; at a time &mdash; five degrees away from where you are looking, vision is a quarter as sharp as at the centre</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Peripheral vision detects a target. Central vision, through the fovea,
    identifies it. A target is first spotted at the edge of vision, then brought into the
    centre to be identified. This is why pilots are taught to make short
    eye movements with a pause on each block, rather than one continuous movement.
    The eye only sees during the pauses, and stays effectively blind
    between them.
  </p>

  <h3>A third of the horizon per cycle</h3>
  <p>
    Nine to twelve blocks at a second each, plus time to check the instrument panel, plus the
    one to two seconds the eye needs to refocus from the panel to distant objects:
    a complete cycle takes ten to fifteen seconds. Over that cycle the
    recommended sector covers <strong>about a third of the horizon</strong>
    around the aircraft, and none of what is behind it. Moving the head widens the
    sector, but takes your eyes away from the one just checked.
  </p>
  <p>
    Lookout is therefore measured by the time between scans, not by coverage.
    Every number that follows comes from that limit.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The size of the target</h2>
  <h3>A grain of rice at arm&rsquo;s length.</h3>

  <p>
    A light aircraft has a wingspan of around ten metres. Here is its apparent size,
    compared with familiar objects held at arm&rsquo;s length.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Distance</th>
          <th>Apparent size at arm&rsquo;s length</th>
          <th>Equivalent</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>0.5 NM</th>
          <td>6.5 mm</td>
          <td>a pea</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>1 NM</th>
          <td>3.2 mm</td>
          <td>a grain of rice</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>2 NM</th>
          <td>1.6 mm</td>
          <td>a pinhead</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>3 NM</th>
          <td>1.1 mm</td>
          <td>the dot of an &ldquo;i&rdquo;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>5 NM</th>
          <td>0.65 mm</td>
          <td>a full stop</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    At two or three miles the target is a grey mark of one to two millimetres
    against a bright or hazy sky. It occupies one block out of a dozen, which you scan
    for one second. Detection is not yet recognition: the BEA measures
    half a second to carry the image to the brain and two and a half seconds for
    the brain to identify it as an aircraft &mdash; three seconds in total.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">The clock</h2>
  <h3>Ten seconds, at a minimum.</h3>

  <p>
    For the UK CAA, <em>&ldquo;it takes a minimum of 10 seconds for a pilot to
    spot traffic, identify it, realise it is a collision risk, react, and have the
    aircraft respond.&rdquo;</em> The FAA breaks the same sequence into steps.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Step</th>
          <th>Seconds</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>See the object</th><td>0.1</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Recognise an aircraft</th><td>1.0</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Realise the aircraft are on a collision course</th><td>5.0</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Decide to turn left or right</th><td>4.0</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Muscular reaction</th><td>0.4</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Aircraft response</th><td>2.0</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Total</th><td>12.5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Two light aircraft head-on at a hundred knots each are still one nautical mile
    apart eighteen seconds before they meet, and two miles apart thirty-six
    seconds before. Reaction time and the scan cycle are therefore comparable
    to the time left before the aircraft meet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Following the scanning technique exactly takes longer still: at ten degrees per movement
    and a second per block, a sector of 180&deg; by 30&deg; takes
    <strong>fifty-four seconds</strong>. The study EASA commissioned on improving
    see-and-avoid calculates this and concludes &mdash;
    <em>&ldquo;therefore usually only the middle part is scanned
    properly.&rdquo;</em>
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">When the target stays still</h2>
  <h3>A collision course produces no relative motion.</h3>
  <p>
    Peripheral vision detects movement. On a collision course, the direction to the other
    aircraft does not change: it stays at a fixed point in the windscreen and grows.
    Peripheral vision therefore detects no movement. The target stays in one
    block visited once per cycle, and its apparent size grows in inverse proportion to
    distance &mdash; imperceptibly for most of the encounter, then quickly at the
    end.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is documented in every safety publication on the subject. Scanning
    more does not solve it. The difficulty is knowing where to look, not how
    hard to look: which block, and when.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="pledge">
  <h2 class="kicker">Directed search</h2>
  <h3>Eight times more effective.</h3>
  <p>
    A visual search in which the approximate direction is already known is about
    <strong>eight times more effective</strong> than the same search conducted
    without that indication. That is the figure measured in flight trials. Electronic
    conspicuity, equipment that makes aircraft electronically visible, has one role: to tell you which
    of the twelve blocks to scan, and when. It does not replace the scan.
  </p>
  <p>
    An aircraft fitted with ADS-L broadcasts its position at least once a second.
    That link ignores the wing, the door post, haze, sun and where the pilot
    happens to be looking. It is continuous over 360&deg;, including aft, where the
    eye has no coverage at all. In the cockpit, you get a
    bearing, an altitude relative to yours and a closing speed. skyBlip warns at 3&nbsp;km, about 30 seconds
    before two aircraft at 100&nbsp;kt meet head-on. In practice, it shows you the glider
    circling in a thermal on your route, the faster aircraft catching up from behind and
    below, and who else is calling in before the circuit gets busy.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Equipment</h2>
  <h3>One standard, and devices that hear each other.</h3>
  <p>
    Glider pilots equipped their aircraft first. In 2010 the Swiss authority recorded that
    around 95&nbsp;% of Swiss gliders were fitted with a collision-warning device
    and concluded that no rule was needed. The equipment had been adopted
    <em>&ldquo;not through regulatory measures, but rather on a voluntary
    basis&rdquo;</em>. French gliding clubs followed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The devices still have to understand each other. In 2016 a glider and a light
    aircraft collided in England. Both carried a device to make them electronically
    visible, and the investigators found that <em>&ldquo;the differing technologies
    meant that the pilots of both aircraft were reliant on visually acquiring each
    other&rsquo;s aircraft&rdquo;</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Europe now has an open standard, <strong>ADS-L</strong>, published by EASA and
    already recognised as a way to make an aircraft electronically visible in
    U-space airspace. skyBlip Go transmits and receives ADS-L, and also receives
    traffic fitted with FLARM-type equipment. Paired with
    <%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "skyBlip Bravo" %>, it
    also receives traffic transmitting ADS-B. skyPost ground
    stations will relay that traffic around the airfield.
  </p>
  <p>
    <%= link_to_page "index", label: "skyBlip Go", class: "cta cta--next" %>
  </p>
</section>

